        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58341,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58342,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Virtually all cases arise in pelviperineal subcutaneous tissue, with the majority arising in the vulva. About 10&ndash;15% of cases are located in the vagina. Lesions in men occur in the scrotum or paratesticular soft tissue.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58343,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most cases present as a slowly enlarging, painless, circumscribed mass. The most frequent preoperative diagnosis is Bartholin gland cyst.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58344,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "<p>Angiomyofibroblastoma is uncommon, having an incidence comparable to that of deep (aggressive) angiomyxoma. These tumours arise predominantly in females, principally in adults between menarche and menopause {1314521; 9308729; 8685229; 24880711}. About 10% of patients are postmenopausal. Convincing examples have not been described before puberty. Rare cases occur in males {1314521; 8980365}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-02-10T09:52:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58345,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58346,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58347,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These lesions are well circumscribed but not encapsulated, with a tan/pink cut surface and a soft consistency. Necrosis is not seen. Most cases measure &lt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;cm in maximum diameter, although rare examples as large as 10&nbsp;cm have been recognized. Some cases may be pedunculated.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58348,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are generally well demarcated by a thin fibrous pseudocapsule and, at low power, show varying cellularity with prominent vessels throughout. Vessels are mostly small, thin-walled, and ectatic and are set in an abundant loose, oedematous stroma. The tumour cells are round to spindle-shaped, with eosinophilic cytoplasm, and they are typically concentrated around vessels. Mitoses are rare. Binucleated and multinucleated tumour cells are common. Some cases show plasmacytoid or epithelioid cytomorphology and rare examples show degenerative (ancient) nuclear hyperchromasia and atypia. About 10% of cases have a variably prominent well-differentiated adipocytic component, sometimes &gt;&nbsp;90% of the tumour {25675192}. In postmenopausal patients, the stroma is often less oedematous and more fibrous, and there may be hyalinization of vessel walls. Rare cases show morphological overlap with aggressive angiomyxoma {9023551}.</p>\r\n<p>By immunohistochemistry, the majority of cases show strong and diffuse positive staining for desmin, whereas there is usually at most only focal positivity for SMA or pan-muscle actin {1314521}. Desmin staining may be reduced or absent in postmenopausal cases. Tumour cells are consistently positive for ER and PR and occasionally positive for CD34.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58349,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:20:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
